Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 18:26:17 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] runbook 2.2.1: RE-KEY the governed sha pointers after every
 base move - mechanize G1 so the class cannot silently return

The second clause of gap G1's owner-sized fix (registers/2026-08-29-release-attribution-audit.md). Re-keying native-grandfather.json fixed the record; this fixes the mechanism. Without it the next base move re-breaks all 289 keys silently and the next seat re-derives the same pass from scratch.

Four numbered steps, because the sha-death class is not one file: native-grandfather.json (289 keys), licenses/README.md (two citations, both DANGLING - on no branch at all), sources.json _base_moved_* (one resolution receipt), and registers/failure-ledger.jsonl (four F-136 binding_fix pointers, and stamp amended_utc or the ledger gate refuses the commit).

Method is fixed, not left to judgment: exact-subject match, assert one unique hit per row before writing, keep the old key as sha_pre_rebase (append, never overwrite), then VERIFY with arifi_sync provenance rather than assuming. git branch -a --contains is named as the check that proves a pointer dead.

Carries the G12 caveat forward: a re-key restores the KEY only, never the CLASSIFICATION, and a green grandfathered-NATIVE count is not evidence attribution is complete.

Origin: ArifiLabs (native)
