 UPDATE-RUNBOOK.md | 28 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 28 insertions(+)

diff --git a/UPDATE-RUNBOOK.md b/UPDATE-RUNBOOK.md
index aad57d003..fa38e08ee 100644
--- a/UPDATE-RUNBOOK.md
+++ b/UPDATE-RUNBOOK.md
@@ -129,6 +129,34 @@ hand: an equal-count whole-file diff on a generated artifact is never a real con
 If the replay is **not** clean, resolve one patch at a time in the scratch worktree, and record why
 in the commit body. A resolution that is not explained is a resolution that will be re-broken.
 
+#### 2.2.1 RE-KEY THE GOVERNED SHA POINTERS — the step whose absence cost a whole audit
+
+**A rebase rewrites every sha on `arifi/main`. Every governed record that names a fork commit by sha
+is dead the moment step 2 above completes, and nothing warns you.** On 2026-08-29 two base moves
+(b10524 → b10636 → b10680) killed 289 of 289 grandfather keys, four failure-ledger fix pointers, two
+license-audit citations and one conflict-marker receipt in one night. `arifi_sync provenance` went
+to `FAIL: 315`. Nobody noticed until an audit went looking. These are numbered steps, not advice:
+
+1. **Re-key `tools/arifi-sync/native-grandfather.json`** by exact-subject match against `arifi/main`
+   over the new base range. Assert **one unique hit per row** before writing — ambiguous or
+   unmatched subjects mean a commit was reworded and need a human. Keep the old key per row as
+   `sha_pre_rebase`; append, never overwrite. Move `range_base` and keep the old as
+   `range_base_pre_rebase`.
+2. **Re-key the other three pointer homes the same way** — the sha-death class is not one file:
+   `licenses/README.md` (take and retention citations), `sources.json` `base._base_moved_*` blocks
+   (resolution receipts), and `C:/ArifiLabs/registers/failure-ledger.jsonl` (the `binding_fix`
+   pointers). Stamp `amended_utc` on any ledger row you touch or the ledger gate refuses the commit.
+3. **Verify, do not assume:** `python tools/arifi-sync/arifi_sync.py provenance` must show
+   `grandfathered NATIVE` back at the full ledger count. Anything else means the re-key is wrong,
+   and you want to know that before the commit, not in the next audit.
+4. **`git branch -a --contains <sha>` is the check that proves a pointer is dead.** Two of the
+   2026-08-29 casualties were *dangling* — on no branch at all, reachable only until gc runs. A
+   reader who clones the published fork cannot verify a citation like that.
+
+**A re-key restores the KEY only. It does not re-validate the CLASSIFICATION** the ledger asserts
+about those commits. Do not let a green `grandfathered NATIVE` count be read as evidence that
+attribution is complete.
+
 Two rules that are not negotiable at this step:
 
 - **Never `git rebase -i` and never squash.** The 1:1 correspondence between a commit and a patch
